/T (Attackers copy trusted brands, create urgency or surprise, and ask for a password, a file open or a phone call. The header samples showed that email authentication gives strong clues: failed SPF, a random sending domain and a signing domain that did not match all pointed to a scam, while a DKIM pass on its own was not enough to trust the message.)
